{"name":"Artemis","hex":"#d2a96e","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Artemis","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The Greek goddess of the hunt, wild animals, and wilderness; the daughter of Leto and Zeus; the sister of Apollo.","A female given name from Ancient Greek, masculine equivalent Artemios."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Artemis"}],"entries":[{"title":"Artemis","blocks":[{"text":"In ancient Greek religion and mythology, Artemis is the goddess of hunting, the wilderness, wild animals, transitions, nature, vegetation, childbirth, care of children, and chastity. She was often said to roam the forests and mountains, attended by an entourage of nymphs. The goddess Diana is her Roman equivalent."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Diane_de_Versailles_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_AGER_Ma_589.jpg/330px-Diane_de_Versailles_-_Mus%C3%A9e_du_Louvre_AGER_Ma_589.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artemis","wikidata":"Q39503"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/0_Art%C3%A9mis_%28Diane%29_-_Galleria_dei_Candelabri_-_Vatican.JPG/500px-0_Art%C3%A9mis_%28Diane%29_-_Galleria_dei_Candelabri_-_Vatican.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/0_Art%C3%A9mis_%28Diane%29_-_Galleria_dei_Candelabri_-_Vatican.JPG/1280px-0_Art%C3%A9mis_%28Diane%29_-_Galleria_dei_Candelabri_-_Vatican.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/0_Art%C3%A9mis_%28Diane%29_-_Galleria_dei_Candelabri_-_Vatican.JPG/500px-0_Art%C3%A9mis_%28Diane%29_-_Galleria_dei_Candelabri_-_Vatican.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Artémis (Diane), the huntress. Roman copy of a Greek statu